        lecture(
            id: "justice-work",
            title: "Work, wages, and economic relationship",
            summary:
                "See labor, leadership, compensation, conditions, and rest as relationships among persons rather than mere exchange.",
            opening:
                "In the economic roles you hold, whose labor or vulnerability is easiest to overlook because a price, policy, or digital interface stands between you?",
            goal:
                "By the end, you should be able to explain the dignity and social purpose of work, identify reciprocal duties of workers and employers, test apparent consent under unequal power, and examine your participation as a consumer or investor.",
            terms: [
                "Dignity of work — the worth of human labor because the worker is a person who participates in creation, sustains life, develops gifts, and serves others.",
                "Just wage — remuneration sufficient and fitting in relation to work, responsibilities, family needs, enterprise conditions, and the common good; agreement alone does not settle justice.",
                "Economic initiative — the freedom and responsibility to create, organize, exchange, and undertake productive work within moral and social limits.",
                "Exploitation — use of another person's labor, need, dependency, or weakness for disproportionate advantage.",
            ],
            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Work is for the person",
                    """
                    Work develops abilities, provides for needs, supports family and community, and participates in responsible care for creation. Its dignity comes first from the worker, not from prestige or income. Necessary work that receives little honor can serve human life more directly than celebrated work. Paid employment is not the only labor: caregiving, household work, study, volunteer service, and the work of seeking employment also involve real human effort and goods, even when economic arrangements value them differently.

                    Because work is for the person, productivity cannot become the final measure of a worker's worth. An employee is not a replaceable unit whose health, conscience, family, and future disappear behind output. At the same time, honoring dignity does not remove standards. A worker can be accountable for competence, honesty, timeliness, safety, and agreed duties. Justice refuses both exploitation and the idea that personal dignity exempts someone from responsibilities freely and legitimately assumed.

                    Catholic teaching affirms economic initiative and productive enterprise. Owners and leaders coordinate resources, accept risk, create goods, and sustain work. Profit can indicate that an enterprise is functioning, but it is not the only measure. A profitable practice may still degrade workers, deceive customers, damage creation, or shift costs onto the public. The moral purpose of economic life is service to persons and the common good. Decisions about production, compensation, conditions, and closure therefore concern relationships, not numbers alone.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — Reciprocal duties and unequal power",
                    """
                    Workers owe honest labor, care for entrusted goods, respect for legitimate authority, truth in records, and fidelity to lawful agreements. Employers and managers owe clear expectations, safe conditions, non-discrimination, respect for conscience, agreed benefits, and compensation that is fair and timely. Colossians speaks into an ancient household order not identical to modern employment, yet its demand that masters act justly and fairly under a Master in heaven subjects earthly power to divine judgment. No role makes another person mere property.

                    James speaks fiercely about withheld wages crying out to God. Pay is not praise or generosity after the work; it is due. The Catechism says a just wage is the legitimate fruit of work and cannot be reduced to whatever the parties accepted. Consent can be weakened by severe need, misinformation, monopoly, immigration vulnerability, discrimination, or fear of retaliation. Unequal bargaining power does not make every agreement invalid, but it prevents the stronger party from treating signature or silence as complete moral proof.

                    Justice also considers the real constraints of an enterprise. Leaders cannot promise resources that do not exist, and preserving employment can be a genuine good. Yet hardship does not authorize concealment or automatic transfer of every burden to those with the least power. A just process gathers facts, protects safety, communicates truthfully, examines who benefits and who sacrifices, uses objective criteria, and allows affected people an appropriate voice. When employment law, union agreements, safety rules, or benefits are involved, qualified counsel and established procedures are part of responsible judgment.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Consumers and investors also participate",
                    """
                    Economic relationships extend beyond employer and worker. Consumers shape demand; clients set deadlines and prices; contractors distribute risks; investors supply capital and influence; institutions purchase at scale. No individual controls an entire system, and moral responsibility differs according to knowledge, freedom, proximity, and power. Still, the convenience of distance does not make every choice neutral. Extremely low cost or impossible speed may be sustained by unsafe work, environmental damage, deception, or unpaid labor.

                    Responsible participation begins proportionately. Learn more where the stakes and your influence are significant. Avoid claims of perfect purity that ordinary people cannot verify or afford. Prefer transparent and responsible alternatives when reasonably available. Treat service workers as persons rather than extensions of an app. Pay legitimate invoices promptly. Do not demand hidden unpaid labor from freelancers or subordinates. Use investment and purchasing influence with attention to governance, worker treatment, and the goods produced, while recognizing that complex financial products may require competent ethical and professional advice.

                    Rest belongs within the moral order of work. Persons need worship, family life, health, and participation beyond production. Neither a culture of endless availability nor deliberate neglect of duty honors work. The just pattern asks what each role truly owes, protects reasonable limits, and shares unavoidable burdens without favoritism. The learner should not leave this lesson with generalized guilt about every supply chain. Choose one relationship where your actual knowledge and agency are substantial, then make that relationship more truthful.
                    """
                ),
            ],
            misconception:
                "Justice at work is not hostility toward employers, contempt for profit, or permission for workers to disregard agreements. It is also not proven by market consent alone. Business viability, contracts, roles, and legitimate authority are real goods, but all remain ordered to persons and the common good. This course cannot determine a wage claim, employment classification, discrimination case, or legal remedy; those questions may require the appropriate professional process.",
            caseStudy:
                "A small business loses a major contract. The owner considers disabling a safety practice for three months because it slows production; she believes the savings could prevent layoffs. Employees do not know about the proposal, and the least experienced workers face the greatest risk. Identify the goods the owner is trying to protect, the duty that cannot simply be transferred, the information and expertise needed, and alternatives that share sacrifice more justly. Why does a valuable end not make an unsafe means acceptable?",
            practice:
