        lecture(
            id: "justice-charity",
            title: "Justice and charity",
            summary: "Understand why love exceeds justice without replacing, concealing, or postponing what is due.",
            opening:
                "Where might generosity be more visible and emotionally satisfying than the quiet obligation that should have been fulfilled first?",
            goal:
                "By the end, you should be able to distinguish justice and charity without separating them, explain why charity cannot excuse injustice, recognize the permanent need for personal love, and order service to both rights and relationship.",
            terms: [
                "Charity — the theological virtue by which we love God above all things and our neighbor as ourselves for love of God.",
                "Justice — the moral virtue that gives God and neighbor what is due.",
                "Mercy — love encountering misery and acting for the person's good; it does not deny truth or legitimate protection.",
                "Structural responsibility — duties concerning institutions, policies, and recurring arrangements that shape persons' access to goods and protection.",
            ],
            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Charity presupposes justice",
                    """
                    Justice and charity are not competitors. Justice recognizes claims that must be honored; charity loves with the life God gives and can offer more than strict obligation. A person may forgive a debt, share beyond a contract, accompany someone in loneliness, or sacrifice time freely. These acts are gifts precisely because they are not substituted for what was already owed. If an employer underpays workers and then funds a public charity, generosity has not repaired the wage injustice. If a parent gives expensive gifts while withholding presence and basic care, abundance has not fulfilled the due.

                    Benedict XVI teaches that the pursuit of a just social order belongs properly to politics and public responsibility, while the Church helps form conscience and animate charity. He also insists that even the most just society will still need love: no institution can eliminate suffering, loneliness, or the need for personal attention. These truths correct opposite errors. Charity cannot be used to leave unjust structures untouched, and structures cannot replace the human encounter in which one person receives another with love.

                    Charity purifies the practice of justice. It prevents another person's claim from being fulfilled with contempt. It seeks restoration rather than humiliation, remembers that the offender also has dignity, and gives beyond minimum compliance when the neighbor's good asks it. Justice protects charity from sentimentality, favoritism, and self-display. It asks whether a gift respects freedom, whether resources were honestly acquired, whether nearer duties were neglected, and whether help reaches the good claimed by the recipient rather than the giver's image.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — The Good Samaritan sees and acts",
                    """
                    In Jesus' parable, the Samaritan sees the wounded man, approaches, treats injuries, transports him, pays for care, and promises to settle further cost. Compassion becomes a sequence of material responsibilities. The priest and Levite pass by; whatever reasons they might have had, the neighbor's urgent good remains unmet. The parable expands the question “Who is my neighbor?” into “Who became neighbor to the one in need?” Love does not wait for social similarity or guaranteed return.

                    The Samaritan's action does not provide a complete theory of emergency medicine, public safety, or social policy. It reveals personal availability and practical mercy. Today an appropriate response might include emergency services, safeguarding procedures, professional boundaries, financial help, advocacy, or simple presence. Love uses fitting competence rather than making the helper central. Entering danger without skill can create another victim; refusing all inconvenience can abandon the neighbor. Prudence finds the form, justice identifies legitimate claims, fortitude accepts difficulty, and charity animates the act.

                    Direct service and structural responsibility should also meet. Helping one injured traveler does not remove the question of why a road remains dangerous; reforming the road does not excuse passing the person already wounded. A food pantry and a just wage address related but different goods. Visiting a prisoner and ensuring lawful treatment belong together. Catholic love does not demand that one individual solve every layer. It asks each person and institution to act according to role, power, knowledge, and opportunity while refusing to use one layer as an excuse against the other.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Mercy remains truthful",
                    """
                    Mercy is sometimes mistaken for the cancellation of standards or consequences. Yet mercy responds to the person within truth. Jesus condemns religious leaders who neglect the weightier matters of law—judgment, mercy, and fidelity—while practicing meticulous observances. He joins rather than opposes them. A mercy that lies about harm cannot heal; a justice that delights in destruction has forgotten the human good it serves.

                    Forgiveness, reconciliation, trust, and restored access are distinct. A person can forgive without denying injury. Reconciliation normally requires truth and participation from more than one party. Trust may need evidence and time. Safety boundaries and lawful consequences can remain. An abuse survivor is not required to resume contact to prove charity. An authority responsible for others cannot substitute private forgiveness for safeguarding or reporting duties. Mercy can desire an offender's conversion while protecting potential victims and honoring due process.

                    In daily life, practice the sequence: first ask what is owed; then ask what love can fittingly give beyond it. Sometimes the same action serves both. Paying a debt is just and may be done lovingly. Listening beyond the required meeting time may be charitable. Advocating for a fair policy may be justice animated by love. Keeping the sequence clear prevents visible generosity from masking invisible neglect and prevents minimum compliance from becoming the ceiling of Christian life.
                    """
                ),
