        lecture(
            id: "justice-mercy",
            title: "The poor and works of mercy",
            summary:
                "Meet material and spiritual need with attention that protects dignity, agency, safety, and lasting participation.",
            opening:
                "When you imagine helping a person in need, is the person present as a free neighbor with knowledge and agency—or mainly as a role in your story about being generous?",
            goal:
                "By the end, you should be able to relate love for the poor to justice, distinguish direct relief from long-term responsibility, practice works of mercy without paternalism, and set safe, sustainable boundaries.",
            terms: [
                "Preferential love for the poor — the Church's special concern for people burdened by material, social, bodily, or spiritual poverty, flowing from Christ's own love.",
                "Corporal works of mercy — concrete care for bodily needs such as food, shelter, clothing, sickness, imprisonment, and burial.",
                "Spiritual works of mercy — forms of care such as teaching, counsel, correction, consolation, forgiveness, patience, and prayer, practiced with fitting competence and humility.",
                "Paternalism — overriding another's agency on the assumption that the helper knows the person's good simply by holding more resources or status.",
            ],
            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Christ makes need morally visible",
                    """
                    The Catechism says the Church's love for the poor belongs to her constant tradition and is inspired by the Gospel of the Beatitudes, Jesus' poverty, and his attention to the poor. Poverty includes material deprivation but is not exhausted by income. Illness, isolation, imprisonment, migration, grief, lack of education, exploitation, and spiritual confusion can create real vulnerability. These conditions differ and should not be romanticized. A person is never holy merely because poor, nor morally suspect merely because needing help.

                    Matthew 25 makes ordinary need a place of encounter with Christ: hunger, thirst, estrangement, nakedness, sickness, and imprisonment. The people in the scene are surprised. Service was not a strategy for religious recognition; failure to see was itself revealing. This passage forms attention, but it must not turn recipients into props. Christ identifies himself with the person, which increases rather than diminishes the respect owed to that person's privacy, freedom, story, and participation.

                    Preferential concern means needs easily ignored receive deliberate attention. It does not mean every request must be granted, that one person must meet every need, or that help ignores truth and boundaries. Justice asks which institutions and persons have specific duties. Charity asks how love can accompany where formal provision cannot reach. Humility listens to people closest to the need and to organizations with competence. The first act may be learning rather than launching a personal solution.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — Relief, development, and reform",
                    """
                    Direct relief addresses an immediate good: food tonight, emergency shelter, transport to care, a safe phone call, accompaniment at an appointment. It is indispensable when delay would deepen harm. Development helps expand durable capacity and participation through education, treatment, employment support, community, legal aid, or other sustained work. Structural reform examines policies and arrangements that repeatedly expose people to the same injury. These levels are distinct, and mature mercy refuses to use one against another.

                    A hungry person should not become an illustration while experts debate long-term policy. At the same time, repeatedly providing emergency help without asking why the emergency recurs can preserve dependence or exhaust helpers. No individual can perform every level. A volunteer may serve meals faithfully; a professional may provide case management; a voter or leader may address housing or wage structures; a friend may remain present through loneliness. Coordination respects limits and gives the neighbor more than one fragile point of support.

                    Works of mercy require competence. Advising the doubtful does not make every confident person a counselor. Visiting the sick does not permit ignoring infection control or medical boundaries. Correcting wrongdoing requires truth, relationship, proportion, and safety. Sheltering someone can affect children or vulnerable household members. Money, transportation, confidentiality, and power can create dependency or risk. Responsible charity uses established organizations and professional help when needed. A boundary can protect ongoing service rather than prove hardness of heart.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Receive, accompany, and persevere",
                    """
                    Jesus announces good news to the poor, liberty to captives, sight, freedom for the oppressed, and the Lord's favor. His mission concerns whole persons and begins from God's initiative. Christian service therefore receives before it gives: grace, truth, the neighbor's account, and the community's wisdom. The helper does not arrive as savior. Christ is already Lord, and the person in need may possess gifts, endurance, faith, and knowledge the helper lacks.

                    James refuses a faith that speaks warm words while withholding necessities. Compassion must become concrete. Yet concrete action need not be dramatic. Regular, reliable service may form justice better than one intense event. Learn names appropriately, keep commitments, follow safeguarding rules, share burdens with a team, and let the organization evaluate outcomes. Ask whether help increases participation and honors choice. Receive correction from those served without demanding gratitude as proof that the act was good.

                    Perseverance requires limits and prayer. Burnout can arise when a person confuses finite responsibility with the role of God, while indifference can disguise itself as boundary-setting. Name the actual commitment: amount, frequency, role, supervisor, end or review date, and emergency process. Keep nearer duties visible. Accept rest. If direct service triggers trauma, compulsion, unsafe attachment, or serious distress, seek appropriate support. The goal is faithful love that can remain present, not a rescuing identity that collapses under impossible expectations.
