            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Examine one relationship truthfully",
                    """
                    A complete course can tempt the learner to audit every failure at once. That approach usually produces either vague guilt or defensive denial. Justice works concretely. Select one relationship or role: worshiper, spouse, parent, friend, worker, leader, owner, debtor, citizen, volunteer, or member of a parish. Name the relevant people, the good at stake, and the source of each claim—dignity, promise, role, law, property, harm, or common responsibility. Do not invent obligations merely because someone has a preference, and do not erase a claim because it is uncomfortable.

                    Next examine power. Who controls information, money, schedules, access, reputation, safety, or the ability to appeal? Power is not itself injustice. It enables service and can be necessary for a role. It becomes dangerous when the person holding it defines every due, hides effects, or imposes burdens without voice or review. Ask whether the same standard would be accepted if roles were reversed and whether the person with least power can safely state a concern.

                    Then distinguish the moral act from the whole self. Describe what was done or omitted, the intention, and relevant circumstances. Effects matter even when unintended; intention matters even when the outward result appears helpful. The app must not calculate culpability, and the learner should not turn an examination into a score of worth. The purpose is truth ordered toward response: what good was honored, what claim was missed, what remains uncertain, and who can help judge responsibly?
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — Prioritize, repair, and build safeguards",
                    """
                    Several claims may compete for limited time and resources. Justice does not promise that every need can be met immediately. Prioritize urgent safety, fundamental dignity, clear promises, dependent persons, and harms that continue while delayed. Distinguish what you alone must do from what belongs to an institution, professional, authority, or community. Prudence orders the sequence; justice keeps another's legitimate claim from disappearing merely because the solution is difficult.

                    Where harm occurred, stop what continues, tell the truth through the fitting channel, restore what can be restored, and accept legitimate consequences. Repair should be proportionate and recipient-centered. An apology without changed practice may place another cycle on the injured person. A payment without truthful correction may leave reputation damaged. Direct contact can be unsafe or prohibited. Confession reconciles the penitent sacramentally to God and the Church, while restitution and other temporal responsibilities may remain. A priest or qualified adviser can help when repair is morally or practically complex.

                    Safeguards convert one good intention into reliability. Automatic payment prevents repeated delay. A written promise clarifies expectations. Two-person approval protects entrusted money. A regular check-in gives dependents voice. A communication rule limits gossip. A budget reserves generosity after obligations. A safeguarding process keeps personal influence accountable. Choose safeguards proportionate to the pattern; do not build a prison of anxious checking. The aim is freedom trained to give what is due more readily.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Justice needs grace, friendship, and hope",
                    """
                    Proverbs prefers righteousness and justice to sacrifice and joins their pursuit to life and honor. Tobit connects remembrance of God, care for family, almsgiving, honest counsel, wages, and the Golden Rule within one father's instruction. Jesus states that we should do to others what we would have them do to us, while the surrounding passage calls for prayerful dependence on the Father. These texts show justice as a way of life, not a collection of isolated transactions.

                    Human practice matters. Repeated truthful acts can form stable habits; wise laws and accountable communities support them. Yet Christian virtue is not self-salvation. Sin wounds judgment and will, grace heals and elevates, and charity gives justice a supernatural life. Prayer asks God to reveal the neighbor we avoid, strengthen repair we fear, and protect us from both self-excusing and despair. The sacraments place conversion within mercy rather than private perfectionism.

                    Write a small rule of justice for the next month. Include one worship practice, one recurring interpersonal obligation, one truth or communication safeguard, one economic duty, and one act of participation or mercy. Each should be observable and realistic. Review weekly without assigning a virtue score: Was the practice completed? What obstacle appeared? Did another person's claim become clearer? What repair or adjustment is needed? Completion records study and practice, not holiness. The course ends by returning you to real relationships where justice must remain firm, humble, and open to love.
                    """
                ),
